        'The fiduciary standard vs. suitability standard is a critical distinction most investors don\'t know about. Fiduciary advisors are legally required to act in your best interest, they must recommend the lowest-cost suitable option. Suitability-standard advisors only need to recommend products that are "suitable", they can legally recommend a high-fee fund that pays them a bigger commission over a low-fee alternative that\'s better for you.',
        'Fee comparison makes the case for index investing overwhelming. Over 15 years, 92% of actively managed large-cap funds underperform the S&P 500 index. Not only do active funds charge 10-20x higher fees, they also deliver worse performance. You\'re paying more to get less. This is why the shift to low-cost index investing has been called the most important trend in the history of investing.'
